      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To determine how many books Melvin will have left over after repackaging them into boxes with 40 books each, we can follow these steps:\n\n1. **Calculate the total number of books:**\n   The total number of books is given by the product of the number of boxes and the number of books per box.\n   \\[\n   1335 \\times 39\n   \\]\n\n2. **Perform the multiplication:**\n   To find the total number of books, we multiply 1335 by 39:\n   \\[\n   1335 \\times 39 = 1335 \\times (40 - 1) = 1335 \\times 40 - 1335 \\times 1\n   \\]\n   Calculate \\(1335 \\times 40\\):\n   \\[\n   1335 \\times 40 = 53400\n   \\]\n   Now, calculate \\(1335 \\times 1\\):\n   \\[\n   1335 \\times 1 = 1335\n   \\]\n   Subtract the two results:\n   \\[\n   53400 - 1335 = 52065\n   \\]\n   So, the total number of books is 52065.\n\n3. **Determine the number of boxes needed for 40 books per box:**\n   To find out how many boxes are needed to pack 52065 books with 40 books per box, we divide 52065 by 40:\n   \\[\n   52065 \\div 40 = 1301 \\text{ R } 25\n   \\]\n   This means that 1301 complete boxes can be packed, and there will be a remainder of 25 books.\n\nTherefore, after packing as many boxes as possible, Melvin has \\(\\boxed{25}\\) books left over.",
